out.push(S('Stage 4R · Report (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE(['Mr Attar had four hundred words of working code and a first sentence that said kindly be informed that the problem has been solved by the needful change: two replies, both about the sentence, and nobody tried the code. Mrs Khoury said that is because the first sentence told them he was not one of them — every word in it is English and the phrase is used nowhere in English, and the reader\'s attention goes to the oddity and stays there. Mrs Tarabishi said but it means what he meant, and it means exactly what he meant, which is why he cannot see it: a calque is not a mistake of sense but a phrase built on the shape of one language out of the words of another, and to the writer it is transparent because the shape is the shape they think in. Mrs Oueidat said it is the one error nobody will tell you about, because a reader does not correct it but discounts you and answers somebody else, and in eleven years she has never had a correspondent point one out. Dr Qabbani asked what the test is, and has a reader ever met this phrase in something they did not translate — not is it correct, which it is, and not is it clear, which it usually is. Mr Tayara asked how to do that from here, and you read it aloud to this room and watch the faces: four will not react and four will, and the four who react are the test. Mrs Ajouz said hers are in the letters she was given to copy, and that is where almost all of them come from: we would like to inform your good selves, kindly do the needful, please find attached herewith, all of them in a template fifty years ago. Mrs Rizk asked what to write instead, and less, almost always, because a calque is long because it is doing with words what the other language does with grammar. Mr Shihabi said a desk cuts them and says nothing, and Mrs Khoury said a desk cuts them and forms a view: the cut costs four seconds and the view lasts a career. Mr Zahreddine said his opinions have one, the said frame, and Mrs Oueidat said that one is different because it is a term of art in English law and a reader of a tribunal bundle has met it, which is the exception that proves the test. And Mr Attar said the thing he will take away is that both replies were about the sentence.']));
out.push(A('⚠ **The last line is the one to leave on the board.** **Four hundred words of code that worked, and the thread is about eight words of politeness** — **and he had written the eight words to be polite, which is what they are for in the language he was thinking in.**'));
out.push(S('Board clinic – typical mistakes'));
out.push(A('**1** ⚠ _the said document_ · _the same has been actioned_ · _your good selves_ → **three inherited from templates and none of them chosen.**   **2** **A shorter calque in place of a longer one** → _by and large_ **for** _to all intents and purposes_.   **3** **Marking formal English as a calque** → ⚠ _I should be grateful_ **is formal and is used.** **The test is _met_, not _plain_.**   **4** **A reaction counted and a non-reaction ignored** → **half the instrument.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 5 · Read'));
