out.push(S('Stage 4R · Report (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE(['Mrs Rizk\'s email ran to thirty-one words: dear colleagues, please find attached our application for the three districts, we look forward to hearing from you. The application had four things in it with no equivalent in the donor\'s system and she had written none of them down. Mrs Tarabishi said the donor read it cold, and cold, and supplied four things from their own world: they read governorate as a province with a budget, fixed clinic as a building with staff, and two comparable projects as projects comparable to this one, which they are not, because they were comparable to each other. Mrs Khoury said this is the genre the whole unit has been building towards — three absences and one instruction, not a summary, because the document summarises itself, and not politeness, which costs words and buys nothing from a reader with four hundred applications. Mrs Oueidat said the instruction is the part nobody writes and the part she would keep if she could keep only one: read paragraph 4 before paragraph 2, twenty-eight characters. Dr Qabbani asked whether an instruction to a reader is not presumptuous, and it is presumptuous when it is about what to think and useful when it is about the order. Mr Tayara asked how long the note should be, and between two hundred and sixty and three hundred words. Mrs Ajouz said three is a strange number to insist on, and Mrs Oueidat said three is what a reader will carry: she has sent notes with seven absences and watched a court use two. Mr Shihabi said so you leave the fourth absence out, and you leave it out of the note and put it in the document at the place where it arises. Mr Attar asked what happens if the document is somebody else\'s, and then the note is the only thing you control. Mr Zahreddine said his are opinions and carry their own, and Mrs Khoury said the covering letter to the insurer is the note and he writes three lines of it, and the three lines are a greeting, an enclosure and a signature, which is the thirty-one words of Mrs Rizk\'s email with a different letterhead on it, and the room should notice that he has been doing without the genre for twenty years and not once been told so. And Mrs Rizk said the thing she will take away is the two comparable projects: she meant comparable to each other and the donor read them as comparable to this one, and the question took eleven weeks to answer with a sentence she could have written in the covering email.']));
out.push(A('⚠ **The last line is the figure to keep.** **She wrote _two comparable projects_ meaning comparable to each other, the donor read it as comparable to this one, and the question took eleven weeks to answer with a sentence she could have written in the covering email.**'));
out.push(S('Board clinic – typical mistakes'));
out.push(A('**1** ⚠ **A note that summarises the document** → **the reader reads it instead of the document.** **Say what the note is not, first.**   **2** **An instruction with an adverb in it** → _carefully_, _properly_, _in context_. **The giveaway, every time.**   **3** **Seven absences** → **a court uses two.** **Three, and the rest into the document.**   **4** ⚠ **An absence with no wrong supply named** → **information rather than a correction that arrives before the error.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 5 · Read'));
out.push(A('**1** ⚠ **It is not a summary of the application and does not repeat anything in it.** **It names three things with no equivalent in the reader\'s system and gives one instruction about the order of reading.**'));
out.push(A('**2** _Read paragraph 11 before paragraph 9._ **Without it a reader meets paragraph 9\'s two project figures first and carries them into paragraph 11** — **and then reads the refusal in 11 as modesty rather than as a statement that no uptake figure exists.**'));
out.push(A('**3** ⚠ **The grading of districts by access, which is done differently.** **It is explained at paragraph 4 where it arises**, because it does not change how anything else is read and three is what a note can carry.'));
